\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\title{Testing Knit}
\author{Urtzi}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

This document demonstrates a simple knit-like workflow for Julia.

\section{Setup}

First, let's load some packages:

<<setup>>=
using Statistics
using Random
Random.seed!(42)
@

\section{Data Generation}

Now we generate some data:

<<data>>=
n = 10
x = randn(n)
println("Generated $n random numbers")
@

\section{Analysis}

The mean is \Sexpr{round(mean(x), digits=3)} and the standard deviation 
is \Sexpr{round(std(x), digits=3)}.

Let's compute some statistics:

<<stats>>=
m = mean(x)
s = std(x)
println("Mean: ", round(m, digits=3))
println("Std:  ", round(s, digits=3))
@

\section{Conclusion}

We analyzed \Sexpr{n} data points.

\end{document}
